<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borjigin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Borjigin were the Mongol clan to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> belonged. His ancestors, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabul-khan">Khabul Khan</a>, led the Khamag Mongol confederation in the 12th century, and after the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> descent from Genghis Khan's branch of the clan became the condition for holding the title of khan across the steppe. The Yuan emperors and many later Chinggisid khans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> were Borjigin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borjigin.jpg" alt="Borjigin" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: AmygdalicGloss · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Seal_of_the_Mongols_1246.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Borjigin, or Borjigids, trace their descent to Bodonchar Munkhag, who is dated to the early 10th century. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, the first Mongol was born from the union of a blue-grey wolf and a fallow doe, and their 11th-generation descendant Alan Gua conceived five sons after being impregnated by a ray of light; the youngest was Bodonchar. Rashid al-Din Hamadani wrote that many older Mongol tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Manghud</a> and Kiyat, were founded by members of the clan.</p><p>Bodonchar's descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabul-khan">Khabul Khan</a> founded the Khamag Mongol confederation around 1131. His great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> ruled it, united the other Mongol tribes and was declared Genghis Khan in 1206, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. His descendants are the Chinggisids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many dynasties that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>'s disintegration were of Chinggisid ancestry, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochids</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirid Sultanate</a>. Chinggisid descent shaped politics: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> had to rule through puppet khans because he lacked it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> claimed it through their maternal line. In 1368 the Yuan were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghon-temur">Toghon Temür</a>, withdrew north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, where the court continued as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> (1480–1517) brought a Borjigid revival, and his descendants held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> khanates, which remained independent until the Qing annexation of 1691. Borjigin nobles went on ruling their subjects under the Qing and proclaimed Mongolia's independence in 1911. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> the Khiyad Borjigids ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed them in the late 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The communist regime banned clan names in the 1920s, and Stalin's associates executed some 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, including Borjigin nobles. The last ruling Borjigin prince, Navaanneren Setsen Khan, was executed in 1937. When the ban was lifted in 1997, some families had lost knowledge of their clan. Around 700,000 people are registered under the surname Borjigin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> the names Borjigid and Kiyad became the basis of surnames such as Bao and Qi, and a genetic study has proposed that as many as 16 million men, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazaras</a> to the Hezhe, may have Borjigid-Kiyad ancestry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borjigin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Borjigin</b> royal clan and a warrior aristocracy stood at the top of society, while common herders did most of the economy's work, and captives, including many skilled artisans, were transferred from conquered lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation was a loose alliance of <b>Borjigin</b>, Tayichiud and other clans, choosing khans at assemblies.</blockquote><a href="/state/khamag-mongol">Khamag Mongol</a></li></ul></section>
